  <div>
    <p>{{ displayURL }}</p>
    <p>Hello World</p>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      displayURL: ''
    }
  },
  mounted() {
    // Access the defined variable
    this.displayURL = process.env.VUE_APP_MyURL;
    console.log('Runtime URL:', this.displayURL);
  }
}
</script>
```

### Alternative: Using `define` in `vue.config.js` (Recommended for Vue 3)

If you are using Vue 3 (which Vue CLI 3.0.5 often targets or supports), the `define` plugin is the standard way to inject environment variables.

**Updated `vue.config.js`:**

```javascript
module.exports = {
  configureWebpack: {
    // This is the modern way for Vue CLI 3 to inject env vars
    define: {
      'process.env.VUE_APP_MyURL': JSON.stringify(process.env.VUE_APP_MyURL)
    }
  }
};
```

**Updated Component (Vue 3 style):**

```vue
<script>
export default {
  setup() {
    // Access the injected variable
    const myUrl = process.env.VUE_APP_MyURL;

    console.log('My URL:', myUrl);

    return {
      displayURL: myUrl
    };
  }
}
</script>
```

